course: dictionary
lesson: 10633
title: Prefixes and suffixes
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can be built from parts. The main part is the root, the word that carries most of the meaning. In «unhappiness», the root is «happy». The «un» at the start is a prefix, and the «ness» at the end is a suffix. Together they make «un» + «happy» + «ness» = «unhappiness».
explain: Each part does a job. «Un» means not, so unhappy means not happy. The suffix «ness» turns the word into a naming word, so you can talk about a feeling as a thing. Unhappiness is the feeling of being sad or not happy.
explain: Look at the y in happy. Before you add «ness», the y turns into an i, so happy becomes happi and the word becomes unhappiness. Spotting the parts helps you work out other words, such as kindness or sadness.
fact: Unhappiness means the feeling of being sad or not happy.
fact: A prefix goes at the start of a word, and a suffix goes at the end.
fact: «Un» means not, so «unhappy» means not happy.
fact: «Ness» is a suffix that turns a word into a naming word for a feeling or state.
fact: Unhappiness is uncountable, so you say deep unhappiness, not "an unhappiness".
exercise: What does the prefix «un» mean in «unhappiness»? | *not | before | very | again
exercise: Which word is the root of «unhappiness»? | unhappy | *happy | un | ness
exercise: What does the suffix «-ness» do to a word like «happy»? | Makes it into a question | Makes it mean the opposite | Makes it an action word | *Turns it into a naming word
test: «Her unhappiness showed in her quiet face.» What does unhappiness mean here? | A kind of food | Being very tall | *The feeling of being sad | A loud party
test: When you add «-ness» to «happy», what happens to the y? | *It changes to i | It stays as y | It is removed | It changes to e
exam: Ruby says «unhappiness» has three parts: un, happy and ness. Which part means not? | *un | unhappy | ness | happy
